From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] pinctrl: sunxi: fix IRQ bank numbering on the A733 main PIO (no bank PA)

prepare_function_table() numbers the IRQ banks by walking the pin table and
counting bank changes, starting from irq_bank = 0 with last_bank = 0. Bank 0
is a valid bank index, so on a SoC whose pin table does not start at bank A
the very first pin already satisfies "bank > last_bank" and bumps the counter
to 1. The A733 has no bank PA (nr_bank_pins[0] == 0), so every IRQ bank comes
out one too high: pins in bank PB get IRQ bank 1, PK gets 10.

That number then feeds two consumers which disagree about what it means:

  - the irq_chip path (mask, unmask, ack, set_type) and the domain hwirq,
    which take the bank from hwirq / IRQ_PER_BANK, i.e. the counter
  - sunxi_pinctrl_irq_handler(), the probe-time mask/clear loop and the
    debounce loop, which take it from the DT "interrupts" index, where 0 is
    the first listed bank

So a PB interrupt arrives on pctl->irq[0], is looked up in IRQ bank 0 and
dispatched as hwirq 0..31, while the PB pins' own hwirqs sit at 32..63. The
interrupt is never delivered.

Bank PK is worse. sunxi_pinctrl_build_state() stores an entry for every pin
carrying an "irq" function:

	int irqnum = func->irqnum + func->irqbank * IRQ_PER_BANK;
	pctl->irq_array[irqnum] = pin->pin.number;

and irq_array is devm_kcalloc(IRQ_PER_BANK * desc->irq_banks), i.e. 32 * 10 =
320 entries. With the off-by-one, PK's pins carry irqbank 10 and are stored at
320 and up -- past the end of the allocation, on every probe. PK's hwirqs lie
past the domain size, so PK never gets a mapping; the irq_bank_map read in the
irq_chip path is never reached for it.

For the A733 main PIO, start both counters at -1, so that the first IRQ capable
bank becomes IRQ bank 0 in DT order -- which is what the handler and
platform_get_irq() already assume. The opt-in is the existing
SUNXI_PINCTRL_A733_REG_LAYOUT flag, which only the A733 main PIO passes;
prepare_function_table() now receives the probe flags to see it. The A733 R_PIO
(PL, PM) starts at bank 0, which is IRQ capable, so it numbers the same either
way and does not need the flag.

`pinctrl-sunxi-add-A733-sun60iw2-minimal-pio`'s map { 1, ..., 10 } is written for this numbering: IRQ bank 0 (PB) names
hardware bank 1. On the A733 the numbering is not an inference. The A733
Datasheet V0.93 starts its GPIO groups at Port B (section 4.1.5.1); there is no
Port A. The A733 User Manual V0.92 spells the IRQ block out per bank (PB: EINT
cfg 0x0140, ctl 0x0150, status 0x0154, banks 0x80 apart from 0x0100). A live
A733 board running the vendor BSP confirms it in silicon: mmc0's card-detect
EINT on PF6 is configured at 0x02000340 = 0xc0 + 5 * 0x80, i.e. hardware IRQ
bank 5 == pin bank PF, reached from DT interrupt index 4 (GPIOF_NS, INTID 109 =
GIC_SPI 77); and the eleven words at 0x02000000 read back as the bank bases,
[0] = 0x0 for the absent PA and [1] = 0x100 for PB. The A733's EINT registers
live inside each bank's own 0x80 block (irq_cfg_reg 0xc0 == bank base + 0x40),
so the IRQ index there is the bank letter by construction.

The A523 half has been dropped. Mainline's A523 main PIO (pinctrl-sun55i-a523.c)
has the same off-by-one: no main-pio interrupt is delivered, and PK is written
past irq_array on every probe. An earlier version of this patch fixed it here
too, by applying the -1 start to every SoC and moving the A523 map from
{ 0..9 } to { 1..10 }. That half rested on code review and the in-tree
convention only -- no A523 board, manual or silicon readback -- and mainline has
since fixed A523 delivery the other way (the DT lists the unimplemented first
IRQ bank at index 0, counter and { 0..9 } map unchanged), with PK still open.
Carrying an untestable A523 change that diverges from mainline's scheme buys
nothing, so the A523 main PIO keeps mainline's numbering and map, byte for
byte. Dropping only the map hunk would not have done that: with the -1 start
applied to every SoC and the { 0..9 } map, A523's irq_chip path would move PB
from the 0x220 block mainline uses to 0x200.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/drivers/pinctrl/sunxi/pinctrl-sunxi-dt.c
+++ b/drivers/pinctrl/sunxi/pinctrl-sunxi-dt.c
@@ -143,7 +143,8 @@
  */
 static int prepare_function_table(struct device *dev, struct device_node *pnode,
 				  struct sunxi_desc_pin *pins, int npins,
-				  unsigned pin_base, const u8 *irq_bank_muxes)
+				  unsigned pin_base, const u8 *irq_bank_muxes,
+				  unsigned long flags)
 {
 	struct device_node *node;
 	struct property *prop;
@@ -206,9 +207,18 @@
 	/*
 	 * Assign the function's memory and fill in GPIOs, IRQ and a sentinel.
 	 * The extra functions will be filled in later.
+	 *
+	 * IRQ banks are numbered in DT "interrupts" order, so the first IRQ
+	 * capable bank has to come out as IRQ bank 0. Start below bank 0, or a
+	 * pin table that does not start at bank A (A523 and A733 have no bank
+	 * PA) loses a number on its very first pin.
+	 *
+	 * Only the A733 main PIO (SUNXI_PINCTRL_A733_REG_LAYOUT) opts in. The
+	 * A523 main PIO has the same off-by-one and keeps the old numbering
+	 * until a fix is tested on A523 hardware.
 	 */
-	irq_bank = 0;
-	last_bank = 0;
+	irq_bank = (flags & SUNXI_PINCTRL_A733_REG_LAYOUT) ? -1 : 0;
+	last_bank = irq_bank;
 	for (i = 0; i < npins; i++) {
 		struct sunxi_desc_pin *pin = &pins[i];
 		int bank = (pin->pin.number - pin_base) / PINS_PER_BANK;
@@ -352,7 +362,7 @@
 		return PTR_ERR(pins);
 
 	ret = prepare_function_table(&pdev->dev, pnode, pins, desc->npins,
-				     desc->pin_base, irq_bank_muxes);
+				     desc->pin_base, irq_bank_muxes, flags);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
